    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. i'll try the blood meal. i was a bit worried about the bone meal though since i heard it's more for phosphorus... but why exactly does blood meal have such a lower impact on the pH compared to other nitrogen sources? could it mess with the soil microbes or anything?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Blood meal is a relatively mild choice compared to some other nitrogen boosters, which is why it's often favored for its balanced nutrient release. It's less alkaline than, say, some synthetic fertilizers, which is why it won't aggressively push your pH away from the ideal range for kale. It's a slow-release organic source, meaning it feeds the soil microbes gradually, which is good for the overall ecosystem, unlike some quick-fix chemical treatments. You don't want to shock your soil life, and the slow burn of blood meal is much gentler on your microbial community.",
